<div class="qandaset block" id="av_1.0-ch19_s04_s07_qa02">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Book:_General_Chemistry:_Principles_Patterns_and_Applications_(Averill)/19:_Electrochemistry/19.05:_Electrochemical_Cells_and_Thermodynamics" class="mt-section"><span id="Numerical_Problems"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-349772 editable">Numerical Problems</h3>

<ol class="qandadiv" id="av_1.0-ch19_s04_s07_qa02_qd01">
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349772" id="av_1.0-ch19_s04_s07_qa02_qd01_q01">
    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349772">The chemical equation for the combustion of butane is as follows:</p>

    <p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-349772">\( C_{4}H{10}\left ( g \right )+\dfrac{13}{2}O_{2}\left ( g \right )\rightarrow 2CO_{2}\left ( g \right )+5H_{2}O\left ( g \right ) \)</p>

    <p class="para lt-chem-349772">This reaction has &Delta;<em>H</em>&deg; = &minus;2877 kJ/mol. Calculate <em>E</em>&deg;<sub>cell</sub> and then determine &Delta;<em>G</em>&deg;. Is this a spontaneous process? What is the change in entropy that accompanies this process at 298 K?</p>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349772" id="av_1.0-ch19_s04_s07_qa02_qd01_q02">
    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349772">How many electrons are transferred during the reaction Pb(s) + Hg<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>(s) &rarr; PbCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + 2Hg(l)? What is the standard cell potential? Is the oxidation of Pb by Hg<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub> spontaneous? Calculate &Delta;<em>G</em>&deg; for this reaction.</p>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349772" id="av_1.0-ch19_s04_s07_qa02_qd01_q03">
    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349772">For the cell represented as Al(s)&#8739;Al<sup>3+</sup>(aq)&#8741;Sn<sup>2+</sup>(aq), Sn<sup>4+</sup>(aq)&#8739;Pt(s), how many electrons are transferred in the redox reaction? What is the standard cell potential? Is this a spontaneous process? What is &Delta;<em>G</em>&deg;?</p>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349772" id="av_1.0-ch19_s04_s07_qa02_qd01_q04">
    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349772">Explain why the sum of the potentials for the half-reactions Sn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Sn(s) and Sn<sup>4+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Sn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) does not equal the potential for the reaction Sn<sup>4+</sup>(aq) + 4e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Sn(s). What is the net cell potential? Compare the values of &Delta;<em>G</em>&deg; for the sum of the potentials and the actual net cell potential.</p>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349772" id="av_1.0-ch19_s04_s07_qa02_qd01_q05">
    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349772">Based on <a title="Home" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Howard_University/General_Chemistry%3A_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_7%3A_Thermodynamics_and_Electrochemistry/Chapter_19%3A_Electrochemistry/Chapter_19.3%3A_Comparing_Strengths_of_Oxidants_and_Reductants#averill_1.0-ch19_s03_t01" rel="internal">Table 19.3.1</a> and <a title="Home" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Ancillary_Materials/Reference/Reference_Tables/Electrochemistry_Tables/P1%3A_Standard_Reduction_Potentials_by_Element" rel="internal">Table P1</a>, do you agree with the proposed potentials for the following half-reactions? Why or why not?</p>

    <ol>
        <li class="lt-chem-349772">Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Cu(s), <em>E</em>&deg; = 0.68 V</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349772">Ce<sup>4+</sup>(aq) + 4e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Ce(s), <em>E</em>&deg; = &minus;0.62 V</li>
    </ol>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349772" id="av_1.0-ch19_s04_s07_qa02_qd01_q06">
    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349772">For each reaction, calculate <em>E</em>&deg;<sub>cell</sub> and then determine &Delta;<em>G</em>&deg;. Indicate whether each reaction is spontaneous.</p>

    <ol>
        <li class="lt-chem-349772">2Na(s) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; 2NaOH(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349772">K<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>6</sub>(aq) + I<sub>2</sub>(s) &rarr; 2KI(aq) + 2K<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq)</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349772">Sn(s) + CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) &rarr; Cu(s) + SnSO<sub>4</sub>(aq)</li>
    </ol>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349772" id="av_1.0-ch19_s04_s07_qa02_qd01_q07">
    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349772">What is the standard change in free energy for the reaction between Ca<sup>2+</sup> and Na(s) to give Ca(s) and Na<sup>+</sup>? Do the sign and magnitude of &Delta;<em>G</em>&deg; agree with what you would expect based on the positions of these elements in the periodic table? Why or why not?</p>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349772" id="av_1.0-ch19_s04_s07_qa02_qd01_q08">
    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349772">In acidic solution, permanganate (MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>) oxidizes Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> to chlorine gas, and MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> is reduced to Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq).</p>

    <ol>
        <li class="lt-chem-349772">Write the balanced chemical equation for this reaction.</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349772">Determine <em>E</em>&deg;<sub>cell</sub>.</li>
        <li class="lt-chem-349772">Calculate the equilibrium constant.</li>
    </ol>
    </div>
    </li>
    <li class="qandaentry lt-chem-349772" id="av_1.0-ch19_s04_s07_qa02_qd01_q09">
    <div class="question">
    <p class="para lt-chem-349772">Potentiometric titrations are an efficient method for determining the endpoint of a redox titration. In such a titration, the potential of the solution is monitored as measured volumes of an oxidant or a reductant are added. Data for a typical titration, the potentiometric titration of Fe(II) with a 0.1 M solution of Ce(IV), are given in the following table. The starting potential has been arbitrarily set equal to zero because it is the <em>change</em> in potential with the addition of the oxidant that is important.</p>

